Windows 10 AND Windows 11 Start Up
1. Change Admin password 
  From the start menu, search for “Change your password,” and select that option. 
  Click Password, then click “Change.” Enter the current password (“kingbob”). 
  Enter the new password from the password sheet. Enter anything for the password hint. Click next, then click finish. Do not forget to check the box on the password sheet. 
  NEW: NMAP IP addresses on network from windows 10 box

2. Disable any potential users 
  - Admin PowerShell command prompt: net user <username> /active:no ;   - GUI: Start > "Computer Management" > System Tools > Local Users and Groups > Users > select user acccount > Properties > check "Account is Disabled" > Apply and OK)

3. Check inbound and outbound Firewall rules, and lock down what is not needed 
  - Powershell: Get-NetFirewallRule -Direction <Inbound/Outbound> ; Manage with New-NetFirewallRule / Set-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName "<>" -Direction <Inbound/Outbound> -Protocol <exICMPv4> -Action Allow
  - GUI: Open Control Panel > search for "firewall" > Windows Defender Firewall > Advanced Settings > you can see/modify inbound and outbound rules 
  - Ports: see active (netstat -a -n) --> verify a port is blocked (telnet <hostname> <port>) = can change ports by adding inbound/outbound rules

4. Disable any processes that do not need to be running
  - Task Manager (Ctrl+Shift+Esc) lists apps running
  - PowerShell command line: taskkill used to kill a task (/F - forces termination[ /IM - kills all instances) -- BE CAREFUL
  - Service Manager (Windows Key+R > services.msc)


5. Find any backdoors (netstat -anb ; Startup (startup folder / Run key, startup folder) ; sticky keys)

6. View hidden files to see if there is anything suspicious, and if there is, change permissions and ownership if needed

7. make sure everything is updated (if the competition allows)


